course: arts
lesson: 24
title: Voices, sound and music in films and cartoons
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Turn the sound off during an action scene and it suddenly looks small and a bit silly; turn it back on and the same pictures feel huge, because sound and music do half the work.
explain: Most film sounds are not recorded with the pictures: footsteps, punches and rustling coats are often added later by Foley artists, who stomp on gravel or snap celery in a studio while watching the screen.
explain: Voices are often added later too: a grown-up can voice a child or a dragon, and the same cartoon gets new voices in each language, which is called dubbing.
explain: Music tells you what to feel: put happy music under a scary scene and it turns funny, put two slow, low notes under calm water and suddenly you are scared of sharks.
explain: People say it their own way: "bruh" is a friendly "come on, mate", "cringe" means embarrassing, and "mad" in Britain means amazing or crazy.
fact: Voice actors can sound much younger or older than they are, and many children's voices in cartoons are done by adults.
fact: Foley artists make everyday sounds like footsteps and rustling in a studio and match them to the film.
fact: Film music is usually written specially for the film by a composer: John Williams wrote the Jaws music in 1975; its two notes, slow and then faster, make people feel danger coming.
fact: Dubbing means recording new voices in another language; some countries prefer subtitles, the written words at the bottom of the screen.
fact: Sound designers make and mix many layers of sound so a scene feels bigger, scarier or funnier.
fact: The lightsaber sound in Star Wars was made by Ben Burtt from the hum of an old film projector mixed with the buzz of a TV.
fact: In some films, especially older musicals, a different singer recorded the songs for the actor; today many actors sing for themselves.
fact: Your recorded voice sounds strange to you because when you speak you also hear yourself through the bones of your head, which makes it sound deeper.
fact: Cinemas can be very loud; covering your ears or using earplugs is fine, and some cinemas have "relaxed screenings", with quieter sound and the lights not fully off.
fact: The same pictures with different music can feel scary, sad or funny.
exercise: Mia's favourite cartoon princess is voiced by a woman of 40. How is that possible? | Voice actors can sound much younger | The voice is secretly made by a 12-year-old girl who is paid in sweets | Cartoons use real recordings of real princesses from royal families | A computer ages the voice down
exercise: How are footsteps in films often made? | A Foley artist makes them in a studio | The camera always records the real footsteps of the actors on set | The actors wear extra noisy shoes on purpose so the steps are heard | A computer guesses them
exercise: Why are the two notes of the Jaws music so scary? | They feel like danger coming closer | Two notes played together are always scary in absolutely any song | The notes were recorded from a real shark swimming under the boat | The music is far too loud
exercise: Dmytro hears a cartoon in Ukrainian and his friend hears it in Polish. What is this? | Dubbing, with new voices for each language | Subtitles, written at the bottom of the screen | A copy made illegally by each country | Two different cartoons with the same name
exercise: Kofi says the sound "is doing all the work". What is true? | Sound makes scenes feel bigger | Sound does nothing much, it is the pictures that matter most | Only cartoons use sound effects, action films use real sounds | The sound in films is only ever recorded by the actors themselves
exercise: How was the lightsaber sound made? | A projector's hum and a TV's buzz | From a real laser sword borrowed from a science laboratory | From a big choir humming together inside a deep cave | A computer, no recordings
exercise: Hamish thinks the singing voice is not the actor's. What is possible? | In some films another singer recorded the songs | Actors are never allowed to sing | Singing voices are always made by computers | Every actor has two different throats
exercise: Why does Priya's recorded voice sound weird to her? | She usually hears herself through her bones too | Tablets change every voice into a funny, squeaky one on purpose | She speaks in a completely different way whenever she records | Recordings raise every voice
exercise: Jack's ears hurt from cinema explosions. What is a sensible choice? | Cover his ears or use earplugs | Sit closer to the speakers | Shout louder than the film | Nothing, loud sounds cannot hurt ears
exercise: Ben's teacher plays happy music under a scary scene. What happens? | The scene becomes funny instead of scary | The scene stays exactly as scary | The pictures change colour | The film stops working
exercise: What are subtitles? | Words written on the screen | New voices recorded in a different language by local actors | Sounds made in a studio by Foley artists after filming | The special music played at the very start of a film
exercise: What does a sound designer do? | Makes and mixes film sounds | Designs the big posters that hang outside the cinema | Builds and fixes the loudspeakers inside the cinema | Writes all the words that the actors say in the film
exercise: Who wrote the Jaws music? | John Williams | Ben Burtt | Hayao Miyazaki | Walt Disney
exercise: What is a relaxed screening? | A quieter, lighter cinema showing | A film you watch lying down | A film with no sound at all | A screening only for grown-ups
exercise: Aidan learns footsteps are added later. What does this tell him? | Many film sounds are added later | Actors never actually walk anywhere in films, they stand still | All sounds in a film are recorded at the same time as the picture | Footsteps cannot be recorded
test: Your mate goes, "Film music is just background, nobody even notices it." What is the best answer? | Music strongly shapes how a scene feels | He is right, music changes nothing | Only cartoons have music | Music is added only so the cinema is not quiet
test: What would happen if you watched a scary chase with the sound off? | It would feel less exciting | It would feel even scarier than with the sound on | The pictures would stop moving until the sound came back | You would still hear the sound anyway inside your head
test: A kid in Liverpool says, "That voice is boss, she must be a little girl for real" ("boss" means great). What is likely true? | The voice could belong to a grown-up voice actor | Only children can voice cartoon children | The cartoon recorded a real princess | Grown-ups are not allowed to do voices
test: Your cousin says the lightsaber noise "must be from a real space weapon, no cap". Which is true? | It came from ordinary machines | It was recorded on board a real spaceship flying in space | It was made by firing a real laser in a laboratory | It is the sound of the main actor humming into a tube
test: In a cartoon, a sound maker crunches celery while a skeleton breaks a bone. What does this show? | Foley uses surprising everyday objects | Celery is actually made of bone, which is why it crunches | Cartoons record the sound of real broken bones in hospitals | Food sounds are banned
test: Priya's friend says, "Your recorded voice is your fake voice, innit." What is the correction? | It is how others hear you | She is right, recordings are always fake versions of voices | Your inside voice is the one other people hear when you talk | Voices can never be recorded properly by any machine
test: A girl from Texas says, "Y'all, this film is way too loud, my ears are ringing." What should she do? | Cover her ears or ask to move, to protect her hearing | Turn up her own headphones too | Ignore it, ringing means the film is good | Sit right next to the speaker
test: Two countries show the same film, one with dubbing and one with subtitles. Which is true? | One hears local voices, one reads words | Both audiences hear exactly the same voices in the same language | Subtitles change the pictures so that they suit the other country | Dubbing removes the music
test: Which is true about actors singing in films? | Some do, some are helped by other singers | Actors always sing every one of their own songs with no help | Actors never sing in films, it is always somebody else | The director sings them
test: A Gen Alpha kid says the Jaws tune is "so sus" ("sus" means suspicious). Why does it feel that way? | The music is written to make you feel danger is near | The tune is copied from a horror cartoon | The sound is too quiet to hear properly | Two notes always sound suspicious in every song
test: Ben thought the music "came with the pictures". What really happens? | A composer writes it for the film | The camera records music from the air along with the pictures | Music grows on the film by itself as it is being developed | The actors hum the music quietly while they are being filmed
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a person perceives a recording of their own voice as unfamiliar. | One's voice is also heard through the bones of the head | Recording devices invariably raise the pitch of every voice they capture | A person invariably speaks in an altered manner when aware of being recorded | Another person produces the recording
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the work of a Foley artist? | The creation in a studio of everyday sounds matched to the film | The composition of the principal musical theme | The translation of dialogue into other languages | The construction of the cinema's loudspeakers
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the sound of the lightsaber. | A projector's hum combined with a television's buzz | It was recorded from an authentic laser device in a scientific laboratory | It was generated entirely by computer without any recordings whatsoever | It was performed vocally by the principal actor during filming
exam: Ascertain the effect most likely to result where joyful music is substituted beneath a frightening scene. | The scene is perceived as comic rather than frightening | The scene is perceived as more frightening | The images themselves are altered in colour | The scene ceases to be perceived at all
